SteamARM: zero the call-return stacks only when a block the invalidation erased pushed entries into them.
An entry's host return address lies in the multiblock that pushed it, and the return block is part of that
multiblock, so an invalidation that erases no pushing block leaves no entry stale. The JIT records whether a
block emitted a push with a real host return address (sticky over replacements; disk-cached code counts as
pushing). Minecraft Dungeons II's protector rewrites stubs thousands of times a second, and each rewrite
zeroed every affected thread's 4 MiB stack under the exclusive code invalidation lock.
STEAMARM_FEX_CALLRET_SKIP=0 always zeroes.

